{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:growth:fermi","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:8e329866b3d4afe66842f69955073b7a4bf5ec74db1f590044126e6edaac229d","modelForecastIds":["sha256:9e32442dd80104ef31ec692efe35319d2d9fb5d07be418ab3e540ee18caa519a"],"modelUse":"consulted","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.9},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.8},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.75}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PPP WEIGHT RECONCILIATION FIRST. OECD-38 2025 PPP GDP ~$73T, China ~$42T, so China = 0.365 of the pair. Rolling forward at my own 2026-31 differential (China ~3.2%, OECD ~1.3%) lifts China to ~0.38-0.40 by 2031. The adapter independently returns ppp_weight_china_2031 = 0.3707 (baseline) / 0.3663 (tech-stagnation), which brackets my hand-built 0.38 — I ADOPT the weight (identified: a weight is a ratio, deflator-base invariant) while rejecting the model's growth rates. Use w_OECD = 0.62, w_China = 0.38.\nTHE HEADLINE ARITHMETIC THE BRIEF ASKS FOR: 0.38 x (-2.7 pp/yr China aging drag) = -1.03 pp of coupled growth from China's demographics alone. Adding OECD's locked -0.4pp per-capita drag: 0.62 x (-0.4) = -0.25 pp. Total coupled aging drag = -1.28 pp/yr, which is coherent with (and slightly larger than, correctly, since OECD+China is more aged than the world) the locked IMF -1.1 pp/yr global figure.\nOECD-38 LEG, growth-accounting sum:\n (1) Locked baseline: 0.6%/yr per capita (post-aging) + ~0.2%/yr population = +0.80 pp aggregate, inclusive of 2015-22 trend TFP.\n (2) AI TFP lift, probability-weighted off AI-Q6's own regime means: 0.162 x 0.75 + 0.472 x 0.35 + 0.365 x 0.07 = 0.122 + 0.165 + 0.026 = +0.31 pp.\n (3) Coupling adjustments to (2): aging as DEMAND PULL for automation (labour-scarcity wage signal raises adoption) +0.05; fiscal constraint crowding out public R&D/grid/subsidy as net interest goes 2%->3% of GDP -0.05; Baumol reallocation into F+Q (hours share ~20% rising ~0.2pp/yr into activities at ~0.7x average productivity level) -0.06 incremental, most of it already inside the locked potential anchor so I do NOT double-count the larger -0.25 raw figure. Net +0.25 pp for (2)+(3) combined.\n (4) Capital deepening incl. AI/grid capex stock: a +1.2pp-of-GDP investment-rate rise on K/Y ~3.0 gives +0.4%/yr K growth, x alpha 0.35 = +0.14 pp GROSS; but AI compute has a 3-6 year useful life and AI-Q4 (66%) puts an impairment/digestion phase inside the window, so net capital-services contribution in 2032-36 is roughly half: +0.07 pp.\n (5) Damages/frictions: DICE-type damages at ~1.7C plus electricity-cost drag: -0.08 pp.\n OECD-38 = 0.80 + 0.25 + 0.07 - 0.08 = 1.04%/yr. Cross-check by composition (US 0.42 of OECD-38 PPP at 1.6%; OECD-ex-US 0.58 at 0.75%) = 1.11%. Take 1.10%.\nCHINA LEG, growth-accounting sum: labour input -1.15%/yr headcount x labour share 0.5 = -0.55 pp; capital deepening +2.2 pp (41% I/Y but a sharply deteriorating ICOR and property overhang); TFP +1.1 pp (automation leadership, >50% of world industrial robot installs, partly offset by local-government-debt and pension crowd-out); education/human-capital quality +0.2 pp; balance-sheet/deflation friction -0.3 pp. Sum = +2.65, take 2.70%/yr. Consistency check against the locked -2.7pp: this implies a no-aging counterfactual of ~5.4%/yr, i.e. roughly today's rate — internally coherent.\nCOUPLED P50: 0.62 x 1.10 + 0.38 x 2.70 = 0.682 + 1.026 = 1.71%, plus ~+0.05 pp from within-window weight drift toward faster-growing China. P50 = 1.8%/yr.\nBAND. P10 0.9 / P90 2.75. Variance budget: AI regime span (Hulten <0.15 vs Breakout >=0.60) contributes ~+/-0.25pp coupled; China is the dominant term (a pension/property fiscal accident at 1.5% gives a 0.57pp contribution vs an automation-led 4.0% giving 1.52pp, i.e. ~+/-0.48pp coupled); AI-Q4 capex digestion -0.3pp; Debt-at-Risk/repression tail -0.3pp.\nMODEL DISAGREEMENT, PUBLISHED AND EXPLAINED (rule 3). Adapter translation: cq3_coupled_growth_pct = (GDP_2036/GDP_2031)^(1/5)-1 over us+oecd-ex-us+china, PPP, 2017 base; identified for a growth rate, not a level. Readings: baseline 2.95%, central-path 2.95%, ai-energy-boom 3.03%, automation-boom 3.00%, tech-breakthrough 3.68%, tech-stagnation 2.30%, tech-plateau 2.29%, ssp3-70 2.62%, debt-populism 1.67%. My 1.8% is BELOW all but one. Four structural reasons, not a preference: (i) the model's China prints 2.85-4.58%/yr (baseline 3.81%) and does not implement the locked -2.7pp/yr aging drag — its china_growth_contribution_pp is 1.41 vs my 1.03, a +0.38pp gap that is most of the divergence on its own; (ii) the production function is biophysical with gamma=0.55 on useful energy, so GDP is largely an energy-throughput function and the model's own electrification/datacenter build mechanically manufactures growth — a Jones/Baumol task-complementarity view denies that energy is the weakest link; (iii) the model has no price level, no unemployment and no demand side that can fail, so the BIS Box C labour-income shortfall and the CQ6-D cell are structurally unrepresentable and the distribution is truncated from below; (iv) the OECD-38 proxy is us+oecd-ex-us, which the adapter discloses excludes Japan, Korea, Turkiye, Mexico, Chile, Colombia and Costa Rica — it omits two of the three most-aged OECD economies, biasing the proxy UP. Telling detail: the only scenario that reaches my number (debt-populism, 1.67%) gets there through the fiscal/credit channel, not the demographic one, because the model has no mechanism to break labour supply at the locked magnitude. LOW-FERTILITY CONDITIONAL (rule 4): the model's demographic path tracks UN WPP LOW (~18% below medium by 2100), so every bound read off it is low-fertility-conditional — and note the perverse sign over THIS window: low post-2025 fertility does not touch the 2032-36 working-age cohort (those births enter the labour force in the 2040s) while it lightens young-dependency, so the low variant is growth-FLATTERING at a 2036 horizon and growth-damaging only later. I use the model's spread as a conditional bound on the mechanical energy/capital channel, nothing more.\nJONES/BAUMOL WEAKEST LINK, quantified. CES across automatable (share 0.8) and non-automatable (0.2) task bundles, sigma=0.5 (rho=-1): Y = 1/(0.8/A + 0.2/N). With A at +3%/yr and N flat, Y(10)/Y(0) = 1/(0.8/1.344+0.2) = 1.258, i.e. 2.33%/yr — only ~0.7pp below the naive 0.8x3%=2.4pp, and the marginal rate at year 10 is still 2.25%. HONEST FINDING: the weakest-link LEVEL cap is hard (Y_max = 5x) but it binds in the 2040s-50s, NOT by 2036; by 2036 it costs the OECD only ~0.15-0.30 pp/yr. What actually caps 2036 is DIFFUSION (AI-Q2's 21.3% says fewer than half of firms even use AI in production) and the PHYSICAL build. The binding inputs by 2036 are HV interconnection/transformer/switchgear capacity and licensed care hours, and both clear by RATIONING rather than price — queues and waiting lists, not shadow wages — which is precisely why CQ1's median-wage test fails even though the scarcity is real. Translation caveat: converting that quantity rationing into income requires my stated assumptions on utilization (regulated utilities recover on rate base regardless of load realization), contract duration (2-4yr equipment backlogs at fixed price), and administered reimbursement (care prices set by payers, not markets). No Fermi arithmetic proves any of this.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:58.958Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"US median generator interconnection queue duration falls below 36 months (from ~60) in the LBNL \\\"Queued Up\\\" annual edition, AND quoted large-power-transformer lead times fall below 18 months per NEMA / Wood Mackenzie trade reporting.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2028\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My weakest-link identification is wrong: the physical build is not the 2036 cap. Raise CQ3 P50 from 1.8% to ~2.1% and P90 to ~3.0%; move ~8 points from B and D into CQ6-A (A 13 -> 21); raise the 2031-36 A-regime and B-regime returns on power/grid and construction by ~1.5 pp each; cut CQ5 by ~3.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD-38 harmonized unemployment prints above 6.5% for two consecutive quarters WHILE OECD-38 job vacancies in ISIC Q (health and social work) remain above their 2025 level.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The mismatch channel dominates and my CQ1 conjunct-1 marginal of 0.55 is far too high. Cut CQ1 from 11 to ~5; cut CQ3 P50 by -0.2 pp to 1.6%; move ~10 points from CQ6-B into D (D 15 -> 25); cut healthcare-operator and ex-AI-equity returns in the B and D columns by ~1.5 pp.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI 12-month average exceeds 2.0% in four consecutive quarters.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My highest-confidence leg (CQ2 conjunct B at 0.80) is wrong, and the Chinese demand shortfall I built into both CQ3's -0.3 pp friction term and the China asset column is misspecified. Cut CQ2 from 30 to ~10; raise China's CQ3 contribution by +0.2 pp (P50 -> 2.0%); raise China-equity returns and cut the onshore-duration leg in all four regime columns; move ~4 points from CQ6-D into B.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Cumulative announced hyperscaler AI capex impairments and cancellations stay below 10% of announced buildouts, AND net external AI software/API revenue exceeds 1.0x annualized AI compute D&A for four consecutive quarters (the AI-Q4 resolution criteria, inverted).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"AI-Q4's locked 66% is resolving NO, which simultaneously restores the capital-deepening term I halved and the r* support I discounted. Raise CQ3 P50 to ~2.1%; CQ6-A 13 -> 22 with D 15 -> 8; raise CQ4 P50 by +0.2 pp to 2.2% (capital demand returns to r*); cut CQ5 from 20 to ~12 (B1 falls from 0.63 toward 0.45); raise the AI-complex and private-credit D-column returns by 6-8 pp each.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"MSCI World suffers a peak-to-trough drawdown of >=20% during which G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal sovereign yields RISE by >=25 bps within the drawdown window.\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My P(hedge failure | drawdown) = 0.38 is too low and the equity-bond correlation regime has already flipped. Raise CQ5 from 20 to ~40 (A also re-rates, since such an episode lifts the MOVE decade average); widen CQ4 to P50 2.2% / P90 3.8% on a larger term-premium term; move ~6 points from CQ6-B into C; cut long nominal sovereigns in every 2031-36 column by ~1.5 pp and raise gold by ~1.5 pp.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 1 fermi seat, effort 'growth'."}}